.\" vmxplore.1 — manual page for vmxplore, the KVM console.
.\" Format: mdoc(7). Render check: mandoc -Tlint docs/vmxplore.1
.Dd September 3, 2026
.Dt VMXPLORE 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm vmxplore ,
.Nm vmx
.Nd KVM and libvirt console for Linux
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl -setup
.Nm
.Op Fl -tui
.Op Fl -once
.Op Fl -connect Ar dest
.Op Fl -rules Ar file
.Op Fl -version
.Nm
.Fl -appliances
.Nm
.Fl -appliance Ar name Fl -vm Ar vmname
.Op Ar KEY Ns = Ns Ar value ...
.Nm
.Fl -appliance-script Ar name
.Op Ar KEY Ns = Ns Ar value ...
.Nm
.Fl -selftest
.Op Fl -only Ar name
.Op Fl -keep
.Nm
.Fl -build-all
.Op Fl -only Ar tiles
.Nm
.Fl -destroy-all
.Op Fl -yes
.Nm
.Fl -sysdiag
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a console for the KVM hypervisor your kernel already has.
It reads the estate through
.Xr libvirt 3 ,
joins it to the ZFS volumes underneath when there are any, and drives
everything with plain
.Xr virsh 1 ,
.Xr zfs 8
and
.Xr virt-install 1
commands.
Nothing is hidden and nothing is invented: every mutation prints the exact
command it runs, and every executed command is appended to an audit log
.Pq see Sx FILES .
.Pp
Two surfaces share one engine:
.Bl -tag -width "vmxplore --tui"
.It Nm
the native GUI.
In the static terminal-only build this starts the TUI instead.
.It Nm Fl -tui
the terminal UI, for headless hosts and SSH sessions.
.El
.Pp
The tool is tiered by what the host can do, by probe and never by licence
check.
Stock Linux with KVM gives the full console; adding OpenZFS unlocks clone
lineage, golden images and rollback; a
.Lk https://kldload.com kldload
host additionally exposes its own toolset.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width "--appliance-script name"
.It Fl -setup
Turn this machine into a hypervisor, then exit.
.Pp
Reads
.Pa /etc/os-release ,
picks the package manager for this distribution
.Pq Debian/Ubuntu, Fedora/RHEL/Rocky, Arch or SUSE ,
installs the KVM stack, enables
.Xr libvirtd 8 ,
starts and autostarts the default network, and adds the invoking user to the
.Sy libvirt
group.
.Pp
Every command is PRINTED, in order, before any of them runs \[u2014] so the
list can be read, refused, or copied out and run by hand instead.
Nothing installed is specific to
.Nm :
it is the stock KVM stack the distribution already ships.
.Pp
The group membership does not take effect until the next login, and
.Nm
says so rather than leaving a permission error to be diagnosed.
.It Fl -tui
Start the terminal UI.
.It Fl -once
Print the estate table once and exit.
Intended for scripts, cron and smoke tests.
.It Fl -connect Ar dest
Drive a remote hypervisor.
.Ar dest
may be a host name, a
.Ar user Ns @ Ns Ar host
pair, or a full libvirt URI such as
.Pa qemu+ssh://host/system .
Estate, verbs, the ZFS join and both consoles all follow.
No agent is installed on the far side.
See
.Sx REMOTE TRUST
for which SSH implementation authenticates what, and the one asymmetry it
creates.
.It Fl -rules Ar file
Grouping and classification rules.
Defaults to
.Pa /etc/vmxplore/rules
when present, otherwise a built-in profile.
.It Fl -appliances
List the Apps catalog with each entry's fields, then exit.
.It Fl -appliance Ar name
Build catalog entry
.Ar name
as a VM and exit.
Options describe the guest;
.Ar KEY Ns = Ns Ar value
pairs configure the application.
By default it waits for the first boot to finish and prints the
appliance's real URL on standard output.
.It Fl -appliance-script Ar name
Print the generated installer for
.Ar name
and exit, without building anything.
The result is ordinary
.Xr bash 1
with no dependency on
.Nm ,
libvirt or ZFS.
.It Fl -selftest
Build every catalog entry as a real VM, audit each outcome, tear down the
passes and keep the failures, then exit with the number of failed entries.
.Fl -only Ar name
restricts it to one entry;
.Fl -keep
keeps the passing VMs too.
See
.Sx Build, audit and destroy the whole catalog .
.It Fl -build-all Op Fl -only Ar tiles
Build every catalog entry as a kept VM named
.Ql app- Ns Ar tile
and exit with the number that failed.
Entries whose VM already exists are skipped, so a second run builds only
what is missing.
Tiles build one at a time.
Each is built with most of the host's cores (less two, at most eight) and
four times its catalog RAM (at most 8G, and never more than the host can
spare), because a first boot is package installs and, for the ZFS tiles, a
dkms compile; once the tile answers on its port it is trimmed back to its
catalog size, memory live and vCPUs at the next boot where the guest refuses
a live unplug.
.Ev VMX_BUILD_JOBS
builds that many at once instead, at catalog size.
Every progress line is prefixed with its VM name so a parallel stream still
reads.
.Fl -only
restricts the run to a comma-separated list of tile names or
.Ql app-
VM names.
The run ends with a report on standard output, one block per tile that
came up: its URL, the upstream project's documentation, the guest login,
root over ssh where the host's ops key was seeded, and every secret the
recipe was given or generated.
In the console the same report is the last thing in the Build all window.
.It Fl -destroy-all
Remove every VM this tool built \(em the
.Ql app-*
builds and any
.Ql st-*
self-test leftover \(em with their disks, data disks, mesh and inventory
rows.
Without
.Fl -yes
it prints the list it would remove and exits 2.
A VM it did not build is never touched.
.It Fl -sysdiag
Print the requirements screen as text and exit: the host's OS, kernel,
CPU, memory and installed versions, every capability probe with its
reason, and the three substrates with their requirements ticked or crossed
for this host.
The same screen opens from the
.Sy sysdiag
button on the Manual page.
.It Fl -version
Print the version and exit.
.El
.Pp
These accompany
.Fl -appliance :
.Bl -tag -width "--password pass"
.It Fl -vm Ar name
The VM's name.
Required.
.It Fl -user Ar user
Guest login.
Defaults to
.Ar admin .
.It Fl -password Ar pass
Guest password.
.It Fl -ssh-key Ar file
Public key file to install for the guest login.
.It Fl -no-wait
Return as soon as the VM is defined, without waiting for its first boot.
.It Fl -golden
Once the appliance answers on its port, shut it down, seal it and snapshot
its root and data disks
.Pa @golden ,
and skip enrollment: a template carries no mesh key, certificate or
inventory row, because a clone must mint its own.
Prints
.Ar vmname Ns @golden .
The same is a checkbox in the build dialog, and
.Sy Clone
on the golden then stamps out ready copies in seconds instead of running
the recipe again.
.El
.Sh THE ESTATE
The left pane is the estate: every domain libvirt knows about, joined to
the storage underneath it.
.Pp
Domains are grouped into collapsible branches by the ruleset
.Pq see Sx RULES .
A branch header carries its member count and how many are running; groups
with nothing running are folded on first paint and never fought with
afterwards.
Each leaf carries two lines: the domain name and its state, then vCPU
count, memory, guest address, backing volume and snapshot count.
.Pp
State is colour as well as text — green for running, dull brown for shut
off, amber for warned, white for paused.
.Bl -tag -width "ctrl-click"
.It Ic click
Select a domain.
The console pane, the dossier and the verb row all follow it.
.It Ic click No on the state dot
Check the row for a batch operation.
A bar appears above the list once anything is checked.
.It Ic shift-click , Ic ctrl-click
Extend the checked range from the last one toggled.
.It Ic right-click
The full verb menu for that domain.
.El
.Pp
The batch bar runs Start, Reboot, Shut down, Force off or Delete across
every checked domain.
Failures are collected per domain rather than aborting the sweep, so one
bad machine cannot stop a run of forty.
.Pp
The search box filters the tree as you type; branches with no surviving
member disappear with their members.
.Sh THE CONSOLE
The right pane carries two tabs, Screen and Serial: the two ways to look at
the machine you have, with Screen in front and selected by default.
Building lives in the estate tree's
.Sy Apps
branch and the
.Sy Build
menu; the substrate's toolset is the tree's
.Sy kldload tools
branch, and a third tab appears while one of its tools is running.
.Ss Screen
The guest's framebuffer, rendered by a from-scratch RFB (VNC) client
directly into the window.
There is no
.Xr websockify 1 ,
no noVNC and no external viewer anywhere in the path — the client speaks
to qemu's VNC server over loopback, or over the SSH transport when the
target is remote.
.Pp
Mouse and keyboard are forwarded, including modifiers; pointer position is
mapped through the letterbox so clicks land where they appear to.
The guest's own clipboard changes arrive on the host clipboard.
.Pp
The client asks for Raw pixels only, plus the DesktopSize pseudo-encoding so
a guest that resizes at boot is followed.
Tight and ZRLE trade CPU for bandwidth, and the peer here is always local
qemu — reached over loopback or an
.Xr ssh 1
forward — so there is no bandwidth to trade for, and no codec to carry or
to defend.
.Pp
Clipboard text from the guest arrives as RFB latin-1, which is the
protocol's limit: a non-Latin character from a guest with no clipboard agent
degrades.
In the other direction the host clipboard is handed to the guest and the
guest performs its own paste, so full UTF-8 needs
.Sy qemu-vdagent
running there.
Every machine
.Nm
builds is given the channel it needs.
.Ss Guest resolution
The console asks the guest to match the pane.
When the pane settles at a new size — a window drag, or entering fullscreen —
.Nm
sends an RFB
.Sy SetDesktopSize
and the guest changes video mode, so the picture fills the pane at 1:1
instead of being scaled up from whatever resolution the guest happened to
boot at.
The request is debounced: a drag fires dozens of resize events and each mode
change costs the guest a black flash and a driver reprobe, so only the size
you stop at is asked for.
.Pp
Two things have to be true for it to work, and both fail softly.
The server must advertise the
.Sy ExtendedDesktopSize
pseudo-encoding — qemu does — and the guest's video driver must be able to
change mode, which
.Sy virtio-gpu
with a driver loaded can and a guest sitting in legacy VGA text mode cannot.
Where either is missing the framebuffer keeps its own size and is
letterboxed to fit, exactly as before: this improves the view, it is never
required for one.
.Ss Serial
A real terminal attached to
.Xr virsh 1
.Cm console
for the selected domain, inside the window.
Boot messages, a login prompt, a headless tty, copy and paste.
It follows the selection automatically: attaching when a running domain is
picked, swapping when the selection changes, detaching when it stops.
.Ss Apps
The catalog, a branch of the estate tree: a self-hosted application
delivered as a configured machine.
See
.Sx APPS .
.Ss kldload tools
On a kldload host, the last branch of the estate tree lists the
substrate's own tools by group, coloured by what they do: green builds,
blue is storage, gold reads, red destroys, purple is a guided demo.
Selecting a tool opens its verbs, curated from that tool's real usage
banner rather than guessed, or runs it in an embedded terminal; either
one appears as a
.Sy kldload
tab beside Screen and Serial for as long as it is up, and the tab leaves
with it.
.Pp
On any other host the branch holds one row that says what a kldload
substrate adds.
.Ss Console controls
The console has no button bar.
Both of its controls are keys, because both used to be icons in the pane's
top-right corner and an icon there sits on top of guest pixels for an entire
session — over whatever the guest itself draws in that corner — to save a
keystroke.
.Bl -tag -width "Alt+Insert"
.It Ic Alt+Insert
Toggle fullscreen.
The chord is configurable — see
.Ev VMX_FULLSCREEN_KEY
below.
The selected pane takes the whole
.Nm
window — the estate list, the details pane, the tab bar, the card border
and the window padding all go — and the guest is asked to change its
resolution to match, so the picture fills it rather than sitting
letterboxed inside it
.Pq see Sx GUEST RESOLUTION .
The same chord comes back out.
.Pp
The window goes fullscreen on the host at the same time and from the same
key, because a console that fills a window-sized window is not what
.Dq fullscreen
means to anybody.
.Pp
.Sy Multi-head :
the window goes fullscreen on the monitor it is on.
Under Wayland that takes a one-line change to GLFW that
.Nm
is built with, since the stock library can only name the primary monitor
there; see
.Ev VMX_FULLSCREEN_WINDOW
under ENVIRONMENT.
On X11 the toolkit's own geometry search finds the right head.
.Pp
It is bound twice, once on the window and once inside the console widget,
because a focused console forwards the whole keyboard to the guest: without
the second binding the key would reach the guest as an ordinary keypress at
precisely the moment it is most wanted.
.Pp
This is the only chord a guest can never receive, which is why it can be
changed.
Every candidate collides with something:
.Ic F12
is the firmware boot menu,
.Ic F11
is fullscreen in every browser, and
.Ic Alt+Insert
generates code in some IDEs.
Pick the one that is wrong for the guests you do not run.
.Pp
The chord in force is printed in the console header beside
.Ic Manual ,
so it is never a guess.
.It Ic Ctrl+V
Send the host clipboard to the guest.
See
.Sx CLIPBOARD .
.It Ic Ctrl+C , Ctrl+X , Ctrl+A
Forwarded to the guest untouched, like every other key.
.Nm
does not implement copy: the guest copies, its clipboard agent sends the
text back, and it lands on the host clipboard on its own.
.Pp
This is worth stating because the toolkit makes it a decision rather than a
default.
Fyne intercepts these three chords before they reach the console widget, and
a client that quietly ignores one does not pass it through — it eats it.
.Nm
hands them back, so
.Ic Ctrl+C
interrupts a process in a guest terminal, which is what it is for far more
often than copying.
.El
.Pp
If the pointer is the only thing to hand, moving it into the top-right
corner of a fullscreen console reveals a restore button; it is invisible
until then.
.Ic Escape
is deliberately not bound to anything: the guest owns it, and a firmware
menu or a
.Xr vi 1
session would lose it.
.Sh VERBS
Every mutation is a plan: a title, an exact list of commands, and a
warning when there is a consequence worth stating.
Plans execute in order and stop at the first failure.
The command appears in the status line as it runs and is appended to the
audit log
.Pq see Sx FILES .
.Pp
There is no confirmation step on any verb, including
.Cm delete .
A VM here is made from a golden in seconds and remade as fast; a gate that
taxes every real deletion to insure against a rare mistaken one is the
wrong trade.
The audit log is the record that replaced it.
.Ss Lifecycle
.Bl -tag -width "Force off"
.It Cm Start
.Xr virsh 1
.Cm start .
Refuses a domain that is already running.
.It Cm Shut down
.Xr virsh 1
.Cm shutdown
— a graceful ACPI request the guest may take its time over.
.It Cm Force off
.Xr virsh 1
.Cm destroy
— the plug pull, with no chance for the guest to flush.
Refused for transient domains, where
.Cm destroy
does not stop the machine but erases its definition.
.It Cm Reboot , Cm Suspend , Cm Resume
The light lifecycle verbs, each refusing a domain in the wrong state.
.It Cm Delete
Forces the domain off if it is running, undefines it including its NVRAM,
and destroys the backing volume with every snapshot and clone under it.
A transient domain is not undefined, because
.Cm destroy
already erased it and the failed
.Cm undefine
would abort the plan before the volume was removed.
.El
.Ss Storage
.Bl -tag -width "Rollback..."
.It Cm Snapshot ...
A ZFS snapshot of the backing volume, named
.Pa manual- Ns Ar suffix ,
defaulting to a timestamp.
This is a storage snapshot, not
.Xr virsh 1
.Cm snapshot-create :
the volume is the truth.
A running guest is warned that the result is crash-consistent.
.It Cm Rollback ...
.Xr zfs 8
.Cm rollback Fl r
to a chosen snapshot, newest first.
Refuses while the domain runs, and states how many newer snapshots
.Fl r
will destroy.
.El
.Ss Configure
.Bl -tag -width "Autostart on/off"
.It Cm vCPU / memory ...
Sets both through
.Fl -config ,
so the change lands in the persistent definition and takes effect on the
next start.
Safe for a running domain, and the only variant that survives a reboot.
.It Cm Autostart on/off
Whether the domain starts with the host.
.El
.Ss Estate
.Bl -tag -width "Migrate to host..."
.It Cm Migrate to host ...
Live migration with storage.
Designed, not yet built.
.El
.Sh BUILDING MACHINES
.Ss New VM
Two sources, chosen in the dialog:
.Bl -tag -width "installer ISO"
.It Cm cloud image
A distribution's own cloud image, configured by cloud-init and booting
ready to use.
Presets cover
.Ar fedora ,
.Ar debian ,
.Ar ubuntu ,
.Ar centos ,
.Ar rocky
and
.Ar arch ,
or a path to any qcow2 you already have.
Images are cached — the family's caches are searched first, so a host
never downloads the same image twice.
.It Cm installer ISO
Any installer image, booted the ordinary way with the distribution's own
installer in the Screen tab.
An Arch live ISO or a RHEL DVD work exactly as they would on metal.
Nothing is special-cased.
.El
.Pp
The dialog also collects vCPU count, memory, disk size, and — in cloud
mode — the guest login.
.Pp
Storage is a sparse ZFS volume when the host has a pool, with the parent
dataset derived from where the existing estate already lives.
Without ZFS the pipeline falls back to a qcow2 file, and everything else
behaves identically.
.Pp
The machine is defined persistently before the dialog closes: a domain
that exists only until the host reboots is a trap, not a VM.
.Ss The guest login
A named account with passwordless
.Xr sudo 8 ,
a shell, and whichever of a password or an SSH public key was supplied.
The key field is prefilled from the operator's own
.Pa ~/.ssh
when a public key can be found.
.Pp
If neither a password nor a key is given, a default password is set
anyway.
An account with neither is unusable at the console
.Em and
over SSH, and a machine nobody can log into is a machine that can only be
destroyed.
.Ss Post-install scripts
Anything pasted into the post-install box is written into the guest and
run once, as root, on its first boot.
Output goes to the guest's cloud-init log and to both consoles, so the
build narrates itself rather than sitting on a login prompt.
.Pp
This is how a stock image becomes somebody's own appliance: cloud image,
plus a script, then Make Golden, then clone.
.Ss Make Golden
Seals a machine into a template:
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
shut it down and wait for it,
.It
generalise it — the substrate's own sealer when present, otherwise
.Xr virt-sysprep 1 ,
otherwise a loud warning that neither was found,
.It
snapshot the volume as
.Pa @golden .
.El
.Pp
An appliance's data disk
.Pq Ar name Ns -data
is snapshotted
.Pa @golden
with the root and cloned alongside it, so a clone comes up with its pool.
Re-sealing is refused while clones still depend on the existing golden,
and the dependants are named.
.Ss Clone
A zero-copy ZFS clone: snapshot the source volume, clone the snapshot, and
register the new domain with
.Xr virt-clone 1
.Fl -preserve-data
so it gets a fresh UUID and MAC addresses over the same blocks.
When the source has a
.Pa @golden ,
that snapshot is used directly and no new one is taken.
Every zvol the domain names is cloned, in the domain's disk order, and
handed to
.Xr virt-clone 1
one
.Fl -file
at a time; a disk the domain still names but the pool no longer has refuses
the clone before anything is created, naming the disk.
Should
.Xr virt-clone 1
refuse anyway, the zvol clones it was meant to attach are destroyed again
and the error says so.
.Pp
Blocks are shared until a clone diverges, so a clone costs almost nothing
until it is used.
.Ss EZ Fleet
One dialog for the whole pattern: build a machine, let it settle, seal it
into a golden, then stamp out a chosen number of clones.
Cloud mode only — a golden must be a finished system — and it needs a ZFS
parent.
.Ss Sound
Every machine
.Nm
builds is given an emulated Intel HD Audio card
.Pq Sy ich9 ,
because a guest with no sound device cannot be given one later without
editing its XML and rebooting it, and the card costs nothing.
.Pp
Whether that card reaches the host's speakers is a separate question, answered
by the
.Sy host audio
checkbox when the machine is built.
Ticking it does two things, and neither is any use alone: the domain's
.Sy <audio>
element is rewritten to
.Sy type='pipewire'
with a
.Sy runtimeDir ,
and the guest gets PipeWire, WirePlumber and alsa-utils installed on its first
boot \[u2014] a cloud image ships no sound stack, so the card alone is a device
nothing can drive.
.Pp
The attribute is why this is an XML edit rather than a
.Xr virt-install 1
flag: libvirt starts qemu WITHOUT XDG_RUNTIME_DIR, so qemu cannot find the
session socket on its own;
.Sy --audio
rejects the attribute, and injecting it by XPath appends a second
.Sy <audio>
element that libvirt refuses.
.Pp
The checkbox is offered only when it can succeed, because qemu treats an
unreachable audio backend as fatal rather than falling back to silence: a
domain wired to one that is not there does not start at all.
The probe runs qemu itself, under the same empty environment libvirt uses
\[u2014] checking file permissions instead reported success on a host where
every domain failed.
.Pp
On a stock system connection it cannot: /run/user/$UID is mode 0700 and
libvirt's qemu is not the desktop user.
An ACL on the socket is
.Em not
sufficient \[u2014] PipeWire still refuses the client.
The route that works is setting
.Va user
in
.Pa /etc/libvirt/qemu.conf
to the account that owns the audio session, then restarting libvirtd; that
also removes the isolation between guests and that user, which is why
.Nm
prints the suggestion instead of applying it.
.Pp
Remote targets never get a host backend.
.Xr virt-install 1
runs on this machine even when building a guest elsewhere, so this session's
audio says nothing about the machine the guest will run on \[u2014] and its
sound would come out of that machine's speakers regardless.
.Ss GPUs
The host's display controllers are probed from sysfs, and over the SSH
transport when the target is remote: the card that matters is the one
attached to the machines, not the one in the laptop driving them.
.Pp
When an NVIDIA card is present, New VM offers to install its drivers in
the guest — the generic kernel with matching headers for DKMS, the
non-free component enabled, and one reboot into it.
The option does not appear on a host without one.
.Pp
Installing the driver is the guest half.
Handing the card to the guest is host-side
.Xr vfio 4
passthrough, which
.Nm
does not do; passthrough is exclusive, so on a single-GPU desktop the
host's display goes with it.
.Sh APPS
An entry in the catalog is data, not code: a cloud-image preset, a sizing
default, a handful of operator-facing fields, and a fixed
.Xr bash 1
post-installer.
Adding an application touches neither the pipeline, the GUI, nor the
tests.
.Pp
Pick an entry, answer its questions, and the ordinary New VM pipeline
builds it — then waits until the application answers on its port and
prints the URL it actually reached.
.Pp
Fields marked as generated are filled from
crypto/rand
when left blank, so the happy path needs no typing and no reused password.
Secret fields are masked in the interface; they are necessarily in clear
in the rendered script, which is written into the guest.
.Pp
Operator input is
.Em never
interpolated into the body of a script.
The body is fixed bash that reads named variables, and only shell-quoted
assignments are prepended — a site name containing a quote, a
.Ql $(...)
or a backtick is inert data.
Values containing a newline are rejected, because the scripts write them
into line-oriented configuration.
.Pp
.Fl -appliance-script
prints the rendered installer without building anything.
The result has no dependency on
.Nm ,
libvirt or ZFS: it is an ordinary script an upstream project could publish
as its own install page, and it lets you read exactly what the button is
about to run.
.Ss The substrate
Every recipe is prepended with a fixed prologue that gives it, inside the
guest, a family-detecting package installer that verifies the artefact
rather than the exit code, a pool built on the appliance's second
.Pq blank
disk, dataset helpers that apply per-workload tuning, a firewall zone
scoped to a source, service enable-and-verify, and counted outcome checks.
A recipe therefore installs and configures identically on an
.Xr rpm 8
or
.Xr apt 8
guest and ends by proving the service answers.
.Ss sysdiag
On a
.Nm kldload
host an appliance is born enrolled: it joins its own WireGuard management
mesh, is issued a TLS leaf from the estate certificate authority, and is
registered in the Ansible inventory \(em all reachable over the mesh while
the service itself stays on the ordinary LAN address.
On a plain
.Xr kvm 4
host the same tile still builds, and its tuned datasets are still real
.Pq the guest installs ZFS and builds its own pool ,
but the disks are
.Xr qcow2 5
rather than zvols \(em no instant clones, no whole-VM rollback \(em and there
is no estate to enroll in.
The catalog colours each tile green, amber or grey for this host and, in
the terminal listing, prints a
.Ql fit\&:
line saying the same.
The
.Sy sysdiag
button on the Manual page
.Pq or Fl -sysdiag
is the requirements screen: the detected substrate as a badge, the host's
OS, kernel, CPU, memory and installed versions, every probe \(em KVM,
libvirt, ZFS and the VM parent dataset,
.Xr virt-install 1 ,
.Xr virt-clone 1
and the kldload toolset \(em as a green or red card with its reason, then
the three substrates side by side with their requirements ticked or crossed
for this host and the same chart, with this host's column outlined.
.Ss The catalog
Each tile builds a VM whose root disk is a clone off a cloud image and
whose second disk becomes an in-guest ZFS pool.
Hardware tiles pass USB devices through automatically.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy Plex on ZFS Pq :32400
