.Ss Build, audit and destroy the whole catalog
.Fl -selftest
deploys every tile as a real VM, audits the outcome \(em the recipe's own
in-guest verdict, the pool on the data disk, the service on its port, a
live mesh handshake, the estate certificate, the inventory row \(em then
tears down the passes and keeps the failures, with each build log under
.Pa /tmp/selftest-<vm>.log .
The exit status is the number of tiles that failed.
It is the same engine the
.Sy Self-test
row in the catalog runs, and the
.Sy Build & audit
button drives.
.Dl vmx --selftest
.Dl vmx --selftest --only \(dqPlex on ZFS\(dq --keep
.Pp
.Fl -build-all
is the kept form: one of everything, named
.Ql app- Ns Ar tile ,
skipping tiles that already exist.
.Fl -destroy-all
is its inverse and lists what it will remove before it needs
.Fl -yes .
Both are rows in the catalog too
.Pq Sy Build all , Sy Destroy all ,
the latter behind a confirmation that names every VM it is about to remove.
.Dl vmx --build-all
.Dl vmx --build-all --only \(dqWeb Stack,AdGuard Home\(dq
.Dl VMX_BUILD_JOBS=2 vmx --build-all
.Dl vmx --destroy-all
.Dl vmx --destroy-all --yes
.Pp
Deleting an appliance by hand, from the estate list or the
.Sy delete
verb, also takes down its management mesh and drops its inventory row.
It did not always: a mass delete of test appliances left every one of
their meshes behind as an inactive member in
.Xr wgx 1 .
.Sh REMOTE
.Fl -connect
points the whole tool at another hypervisor.
The estate read, every verb, the ZFS join, the serial console and the
graphical console all follow.
Nothing is installed on the far side.
.Pp
Only the ZFS mutations are elevated, and only locally; the libvirt calls
are not wrapped in
.Xr sudo 8 ,
because running them as root on a remote target would use root's SSH keys
and a connection the operator never authorised.
.Sh REMOTE TRUST
A remote hypervisor is a security boundary, and two SSH implementations
are involved.
.Pp
The estate read is
.Xr libvirt 3 Ns 's
Go client speaking its own SSH.
It verifies the host against
.Pa ~/.ssh/known_hosts
and fails closed on an unknown or changed key; only
.Ar ?no_verify=1
in the URI opts out.
It does
.Em not
read
.Pa ~/.ssh/config ,
so a
.Ic Host
alias,
.Ic IdentityFile ,
.Ic User
or
.Ic ProxyJump
there is invisible to it: it offers the agent, then
.Pa ~/.ssh/identity ,
.Pa id_dsa ,
.Pa id_ecdsa ,
.Pa id_ed25519 ,
.Pa id_rsa
in that order.
.Pp
Everything that shells out — the ZFS reads and mutations, and the
console's
.Fl L
forward — runs the system
.Xr ssh 1
with the policy stated rather than inherited:
.Ic BatchMode=yes
so it fails instead of waiting at a prompt no GUI can answer,
.Ic StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new
so a first connection is trusted, a
.Em changed
key is refused, and an ambient
.Ic StrictHostKeyChecking no
in the operator's config cannot silently downgrade the channel that runs
.Cm zfs destroy Fl r ,
and
.Ic ConnectTimeout=10
so a host that has gone away cannot wedge the estate's ZFS tick.
Each remote command is passed as a single shell-quoted word, so a dataset
or snapshot name is never re-parsed by the far side's shell.
.Pp
The asymmetry is worth knowing: if
.Ic ssh Ar myhost
works only because of an
.Pa ~/.ssh/config
stanza, then
.Fl -connect Ar myhost
may still fail to authenticate.
Connect with the real
.Ar user Ns @ Ns Ar hostname
in that case.
.Sh CLIPBOARD
Copying inside a guest puts the text on the host clipboard.
.Pp
Pasting into a guest sets the guest's clipboard and sends it one
.Ic ctrl-V ,
so the guest performs its own paste: atomic, and correct for its keyboard
layout.
Every machine
.Nm
builds is given the virtio-serial channel this needs, and appliances
install the guest agent that reads it.
.Pp
An imported image with no agent will ignore the channel.
A paste that does nothing is recoverable; a paste that inserts mangled
text is not.
.Sh RULES
A rules file classifies snapshots and groups domains.
Lines are:
.Bl -tag -width "group <regex> <label>"
.It Cm snap noise Ar prefix
Snapshots with this prefix are automation, and are kept out of the
operator-visible count.
.It Cm group Ar regex Ar label
Domains matching
.Ar regex
are collected under
.Ar label .
.El
.Pp
Without a site naming convention every domain is its own story, so the
default profile groups nothing and everything lands in
.Ar ungrouped .
Copy the built-in profile to
.Pa /etc/vmxplore/rules
and add
.Cm group
lines to teach it your convention.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "VMX_FULLSCREEN_KEY"
.It Ev VMX_BUILD_JOBS
How many tiles
.Fl -build-all
builds at once.
Unset, one: each tile gets the host's spare cores and RAM while it installs.
Set to N, N tiles build together at their catalog size and share the host.
Until 2026-09-04 the count was derived from free memory; that launched five
on a 32G desktop and the OOM killer ended the run at seven of twelve.
.It Ev VMX_FULLSCREEN_KEY
The fullscreen chord, as modifiers and a key joined by
.Ql + ,
case-insensitive — for example
.Ql alt+delete ,
.Ql ctrl+alt+f
or
.Ql ctrl+shift+f .
Defaults to
.Ql alt+insert .
At least one modifier is required: a bare key would be taken from the guest
with no way left to send it.
.Pp
Two families of chord are refused at startup because the toolkit never
delivers them, however well they parse.
Anything whose only modifier is
.Ic Shift
never arrives as a custom binding at all, so the key would go to the guest
instead; and
.Ic Ctrl
with
.Ic Z , Y , V , C , X , A
or
.Ic Insert
is one of the toolkit's own editing shortcuts and is rewritten before any
custom binding is consulted.
Adding a second modifier takes a chord back out of that set, so
.Ql ctrl+shift+c
is bindable where
.Ql ctrl+c
is not.
.Pp
A value that is unparseable or undeliverable warns on standard error and
falls back to the default, because a typo here must not cost the only way
out of a fullscreen console.
.It Ev VMX_FULLSCREEN_WINDOW
.Ql always
or
.Ql never :
whether the fullscreen chord also puts the window itself fullscreen, as well
as collapsing the panes.
.Pp
Defaults to on everywhere, on the monitor the window is on.
Under Wayland the protocol never tells a client where its own window is and
stock GLFW fullscreens onto the primary monitor instead, so
.Nm
is built against a copy of GLFW with a one-line change that lets the
compositor pick the output; see
.Pa third_party/glfw/VMXPLORE-PATCH.md
in the source tree.
Set
.Ql never
to keep the window frame as it is and collapse only the panes.
.It Ev VMX_THEME
.Ql dark
or
.Ql light ,
overriding the detected terminal background.
.It Ev VMX_SSH_USER
The login the TUI's ssh-to-guest verb uses.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "~/.local/state/vmxplore/vmx.log"
.It Pa /var/log/kldload/vmx.log
Audit log: when, who, the exact command, and its exit code.
.It Pa ~/.local/state/vmxplore/vmx.log
The same, when the system path is not writable.
.It Pa /etc/vmxplore/rules
Grouping and classification rules.
.It Pa /var/lib/vmxplore/images
Downloaded cloud images.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Fl -selftest
and
.Fl -build-all
exit with the number of catalog entries that failed, so 0 is a clean
catalog.
.Fl -destroy-all
without
.Fl -yes
exits 2 after printing what it would remove.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Ss Everyday
Print the estate once, for a script or a cron check:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
vmx --once
.Ed
.Pp
Drive a headless hypervisor over SSH, with both consoles following:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
vmx --connect qemu+ssh://fiend.example.net/system
vmx --connect user@fiend.example.net
vmx --connect fiend
.Ed
.Pp
List the catalog with every entry's fields, then build one and wait for
its URL:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
vmx --appliances
vmx --appliance WriteFreely --vm blog \e
    WF_SITE_NAME="My Blog" WF_ADMIN_USER=matt
.Ed
.Pp
Read the installer an entry would run, without building anything:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
vmx --appliance-script WriteFreely > writefreely-install.sh
.Ed
.Pp
Prove the whole catalog, keep one of everything, then clear it all away:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
vmx --selftest
vmx --build-all
vmx --destroy-all          # lists app-* and st-* VMs, exits 2
vmx --destroy-all --yes
.Ed
.Pp
Ask a box what it can do before copying the binary onto it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
vmx --sysdiag
.Ed
.Ss Post-install scripts
Anything in the post-install box is written into the guest and run once,
as root, on the first boot.
Output lands in the guest's cloud-init log and on both consoles, so you
can watch it work.
.Pp
Start every one of them the same way.
.Sy set -Eeuo pipefail
means a failed step stops the script instead of quietly leaving a
half-built machine that looks finished:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -Eeuo pipefail
.Ed
.Pp
.Sy Packages and a shell you can live in .
The guest login is the account you named in the dialog; the script runs as
root, so anything written into a home directory has to be given back to
its owner:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -Eeuo pipefail

export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
apt-get update
apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \e
    vim tmux git curl htop ripgrep jq rsync

u=admin                          # the guest login from the dialog
h=$(getent passwd "$u" | cut -d: -f6)

cat >>"$h/.bashrc" <<'RC'
# --- added at first boot -------------------------------------------
export EDITOR=vim
export HISTSIZE=100000 HISTFILESIZE=100000
export HISTCONTROL=ignoreboth:erasedups
shopt -s histappend checkwinsize

alias ll='ls -alh --color=auto'
alias ..='cd ..'
alias g=git
PS1='\eu@\eh:\ew\e$ '
RC

cat >"$h/.vimrc" <<'VIM'
set nocompatible
syntax on
filetype plugin indent on
set number relativenumber
set expandtab shiftwidth=4 tabstop=4
set ignorecase smartcase incsearch hlsearch
set undofile undodir=~/.vim/undo
set mouse=a
VIM

install -d -o "$u" -g "$u" "$h/.vim/undo"
chown "$u:$u" "$h/.bashrc" "$h/.vimrc"
.Ed
.Pp
.Sy A web server .
Install it, write a page, and leave the service enabled so the machine
comes back serving after a reboot:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -Eeuo pipefail

export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
apt-get update
apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends nginx

cat >/var/www/html/index.html <<'HTML'
<!doctype html>
<title>it works</title>
<h1>built on first boot</h1>
HTML

systemctl enable --now nginx
echo "serving on http://$(hostname -I | awk '{print $1}')/"
.Ed
.Pp
.Sy A container host .
Docker's own repository, because a distribution's package is usually
older than the documentation people will follow:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -Eeuo pipefail

export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
apt-get update
apt-get install -y ca-certificates curl gnupg

install -m 0755 -d /etc/apt/keyrings
curl -fsSL https://download.docker.com/linux/debian/gpg |
    gpg --dearmor -o /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.gpg
chmod a+r /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.gpg

\&. /etc/os-release
echo "deb [signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/docker.gpg]" \e
     "https://download.docker.com/linux/debian $VERSION_CODENAME stable" \e
     >/etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.list

apt-get update
apt-get install -y docker-ce docker-ce-cli containerd.io \e
    docker-buildx-plugin docker-compose-plugin
usermod -aG docker admin
systemctl enable --now docker
.Ed
.Pp
.Sy A development box .
Toolchains that move faster than the distribution ships them are worth
pinning by hand:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -Eeuo pipefail

GO_VERSION=1.26.0

export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
apt-get update
apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \e
    build-essential git curl ca-certificates

tar_name="go${GO_VERSION}.linux-amd64.tar.gz"
curl -fsSLO "https://go.dev/dl/${tar_name}"
rm -rf /usr/local/go
tar -C /usr/local -xzf "$tar_name"
rm -f "$tar_name"

cat >/etc/profile.d/go.sh <<'SH'
export PATH=$PATH:/usr/local/go/bin:$HOME/go/bin
SH
chmod 0644 /etc/profile.d/go.sh
/usr/local/go/bin/go version
.Ed
.Pp
.Sy Locking SSH down .
Only ever do this when a key was supplied in the dialog — turning off
password authentication on a machine whose only credential is a password
locks you out of your own VM:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -Eeuo pipefail

u=admin
if [ -s "/home/$u/.ssh/authorized_keys" ]; then
    cat >/etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/90-hardening.conf <<'CONF'
PasswordAuthentication no
PermitRootLogin no
KbdInteractiveAuthentication no
CONF
    systemctl restart ssh
    echo "password auth disabled; key auth only"
else
    echo "WARNING: no authorized_keys — leaving password auth ON" >&2
fi
.Ed
.Pp
.Sy Making the result reproducible .
End a script by recording what it built.
The next person to open the machine — including you, months later — reads
this before they read the script:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
cat >/etc/motd <<EOF
Built by vmxplore on $(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%MZ)
  role     : web front end
  installed: nginx, vim, tmux, ripgrep
  see      : /var/log/cloud-init-output.log
EOF
.Ed
.Ss From one machine to a fleet
The whole pattern, in order.
Build one machine with a post-install script, check it by hand, then seal
and stamp:
.Bl -enum
.It
Build with
.Cm New VM ,
cloud image, pasting the post-install script.
.It
Open the Screen or Serial tab and watch it install.
.It
Log in, confirm it is what you wanted, fix the script if it is not, and
rebuild.
A script is cheap to re-run; a machine you patched by hand is not
reproducible.
.It
.Cm Make Golden .
The machine is shut down, generalised and snapshotted as
.Pa @golden .
.It
.Cm Clone
as many as you need.
Each is a zero-copy ZFS clone: instant, and sharing blocks with the
golden until it diverges.
.El
.Pp
.Cm EZ Fleet
does all five in one dialog when you already trust the script.
.Ss A rules file
Copy the built-in profile and teach it your naming convention:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# /etc/vmxplore/rules

# automation that should not count as an operator snapshot
snap noise autosnap_
snap noise zfs-auto-snap
snap noise syncoid_

# the estate, grouped the way you think about it
group ^web-        web tier
group ^db-         databases
group ^k8s-(cp|w)- kubernetes
group ^build-      CI runners
.Ed
